{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Two bit planes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" The sources and the targets are each an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"IncidenceMatrix"},{"type":"text","text":", as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"SimpleDirectedHypergraphs.jl keeps two matrices, so a vertex can be on both sides of a column."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Its entry is then target − source = "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"0"}]},{"type":"text","text":", with both bits kept: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"sources(ofHyperedgeAt:)"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"targets(ofHyperedgeAt:)"},{"type":"text","text":", the stars and equality read the planes, so nothing is lost. A"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"directed self-loop is such a column, and its zero matches NetworkX’s oriented loop column and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"HyperGraphs.jl’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"−1 + 1"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"reversed"},{"type":"text","text":" swaps the planes (every entry negated) and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"transposed()"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"transposes each (the dual), both in O(1)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Storage is 4·n·m\/8 bytes, so, as for ","type":"text"},{"code":"IncidenceMatrix","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", this is for dense or small incidence;","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"IncidenceList","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the sparse representation. Equality compares both planes cell for cell, with","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"equal shape, and column order counts.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix"]}],"sections":[],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The oriented incidence matrix of a directed hypergraph: entry (v, p) is −1 when "},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is a source"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of column "},{"code":"p","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", +1 when it is a target, and 0 otherwise. Gallo, Longo, Pallottino and Nguyen"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(1993) define a directed hypergraph’s matrix this way (aᵢⱼ = −1 for a tail node, 1 for a head"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"node); a directed graph’s edge is the column with one source and one target, the matrix of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"spectral graph theory (B·Bᵀ = L, Godsil–Royle §8.3) and NetworkX’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"incidence_matrix(oriented=True)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". It conforms to "},{"code":"DirectedHypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so the "},{"code":"Hypergraphs","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"algorithms (B- and F-connection, components, the clique expansion) run on it unchanged; it is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"not a "},{"code":"DirectedGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"metadata":{"symbolKind":"struct","title":"OrientedIncidence